<div class="wrap"><header class="hero"><div class="hero-main panel"><div class="eyebrow">Unlisted SuperAda product brief · v1</div><h1>Medical Captive Renewal Operations</h1><p class="thesis">The product wedge is not customer support AI. It is a renewal operations control layer: one source sheet, consistent proposal variants, broker selection, locked final PDF, delivery, retained legacy copy, and retired alternates.</p><div class="pillrow"><span class="pill">Renewal ops first</span><span class="pill">Broker proposal portal</span><span class="pill">Source-of-truth control</span><span class="pill">No model training on customer data</span><span class="pill">Ugly MVP, fast proof</span></div><div class="copybar"><button onclick="location.href='#next-steps'">Jump to next steps</button><button class="secondary" onclick="window.print()">Print / Save PDF</button></div></div><aside class="hero-side panel"><div class="metric"><b>35x/wk</b><span>Key spreadsheet frequency, up from about twice weekly.</span></div><div class="metric"><b>2,500</b><span>Approximate spreadsheet lines, up from about 200.</span></div><div class="metric"><b>150</b><span>Potential proposal outputs from 30 entities with 5 variants.</span></div><div class="metric"><b>1.5h</b><span>Approximate review/generate/proof/authorize/send burden per output.</span></div></aside></header></div>
<nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a href="#problem">Problem</a><a href="#customer">Customer</a><a href="#why-now">Why Now</a><a href="#current-workflow">Current Workflow</a><a href="#desired-workflow">Desired Workflow</a><a href="#wedge-mvp">Wedge / MVP</a><a href="#non-goals">Non-Goals</a><a href="#risks">Risks</a><a href="#falsifiers">Falsifiers</a><a href="#key-questions">Key Questions</a><a href="#10-name-ideas">10 Name Ideas</a><a href="#next-steps">Next Steps</a></div></nav>
<main class="wrap"><div class="grid grid-2"><div class="callout panel"><span class="tag go">Recommendation</span><h3>Lead with renewal ops</h3><p>Build the broker proposal portal and generation/distribution workflow before touching intake scoring or customer-service agents.</p></div><div class="callout panel"><span class="tag private">Boundary</span><h3>Data trust is part of the product</h3><p>Support erase commitments, avoid model training on customer data, and keep privileged/protected information handling explicit.</p></div></div>
<section id="problem" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">01</div><h2>Problem</h2><p>Renewal season is breaking under scale. Medical Captive grew from roughly $15M over ten years to $25M in one year, and expects to approach $40M next year. The work that used to fit in a small team no longer does. Andrew used to build a key spreadsheet twice a week; he now builds it about 35 times a week, and that sheet grew from about 200 lines to nearly 2,500.</p><p>Many renewals produce 3 to 5 separate proposals per entity. At 30 entities and 5 proposals each, that is 150 outputs. If each output takes about 1.5 hours to review, generate, proofread, authorize, and send, August and September run out of hours.</p><p>Different team members produce different outputs from the same sheet because of individual bias and skill differences, and handing a renewal from one person to another creates muddiness during variance handling. The spreadsheet is a legacy marker, not the path forward.</p></section>
<section id="customer" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">02</div><h2>Customer</h2><p>The first customer is Medical Captive operations, with Andrew as the operator pain source and likely product champion. The users are the internal renewal team, brokers reviewing proposal options, and eventually clients receiving the final package.</p><p>The buyer concern is not just speed. It is scalability, consistency, source-of-truth control, and trust around privileged or protected data.</p></section>
<section id="why-now" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">03</div><h2>Why Now</h2><ul><li>Renewal volume is rising faster than manual capacity.</li><li>Headcount already moved from roughly 1.3 FTE to 6 FTE and may double again over 2 to 3 years.</li><li>The painful season is fresh in September, October, and November.</li><li>The perfect-world target is to have something ready to roll around June 2027.</li><li>Another provider is already working on intake/checklist scoring, so the open wedge is downstream renewal generation and distribution.</li></ul></section>
<section id="current-workflow" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">04</div><h2>Current Workflow</h2><ol><li>Packets arrive through email and are handled by a back office.</li><li>The team checks information against must-haves, want-haves, and good-to-haves.</li><li>Renewal data is gathered through May, initial indications are created, and data through July firms up renewals.</li><li>Clients often ask the team to also review August, September, and October data.</li><li>The team produces multiple proposal variants per entity.</li><li>Outputs are reviewed, generated, proofread, authorized, sent, and preserved.</li><li>Revision handling creates risk when multiple people touch the same sheet and proposal variants remain in circulation.</li></ol></section>
<section id="desired-workflow" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">05</div><h2>Desired Workflow</h2><p>A secure broker portal becomes the renewal source of truth. Medical Captive creates a 1.0 renewal package. Each later iteration or modification becomes a controlled option in the system, ideally as a dropdown or versioned choice.</p><p>The broker reviews proposal options, selects one, and pushes a button to generate the final PDF. The system sends the final PDF to the broker mailbox and client, keeps a legacy copy for Medical Captive, and retires non-selected options so wrong dollar amounts, wrong columns, and stale revisions do not contaminate the final package.</p></section>
<section id="wedge-mvp" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">06</div><h2>Wedge / MVP</h2><p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> build a narrow, ugly MVP for renewal operations and broker proposal selection. Do not lead with customer support AI. Do not lead with intake scoring.</p><ul><li><strong>Input:</strong> one renewal data source or controlled upload from the existing spreadsheet workflow.</li><li><strong>Variant creation:</strong> generate and label 3 to 5 proposal options per entity.</li><li><strong>Broker review:</strong> secure portal page where broker can view proposal options.</li><li><strong>Selection:</strong> broker selects the final option.</li><li><strong>Finalization:</strong> system locks the chosen option, generates a final PDF, and marks alternatives retired.</li><li><strong>Delivery:</strong> send final PDF to broker and client email destinations.</li><li><strong>Archive:</strong> preserve the selected legacy copy for Medical Captive.</li><li><strong>Deletion posture:</strong> support customer data deletion when long-term retention is not required.</li></ul></section>
<section id="non-goals" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">07</div><h2>Non-Goals</h2><ul><li>No customer-facing support agent as the first wedge.</li><li>No broad AI transformation pitch.</li><li>No replacement of the intake/checklist-scoring work another provider is already doing.</li><li>No model training on Medical Captive customer data.</li><li>No attempt to build the full dream system before proving one renewal path.</li></ul></section>
<section id="risks" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">08</div><h2>Risks</h2><ul><li><strong>Data trust risk:</strong> Andrew is sensitive to privileged/protected data and asked about bulk erasure.</li><li><strong>Workflow fit risk:</strong> if the MVP cannot match the actual renewal variance logic, the team will fall back to spreadsheets.</li><li><strong>Scope risk:</strong> trying to solve intake, scoring, proposal generation, portal review, delivery, and archive all at once will slow proof.</li><li><strong>Adoption risk:</strong> if internal users cannot trust version control and final-option locking, the source-of-truth value disappears.</li><li><strong>Timing risk:</strong> waiting until the next renewal season may miss the moment when pain is most vivid.</li></ul></section>
<section id="falsifiers" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">09</div><h2>Falsifiers</h2><ul><li>Brokers do not want to review or select proposal options in a portal.</li><li>The real bottleneck is upstream intake/checklist scoring, not proposal generation and distribution.</li><li>Medical Captive needs long-term retention or data handling rules that conflict with the proposed deletion posture.</li><li>The proposal variants are too bespoke for a simple controlled workflow.</li><li>The team cannot define a narrow renewal path that represents enough value for an MVP.</li></ul></section>
<section id="key-questions" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">10</div><h2>Key Questions</h2><ol><li>What is the narrowest renewal type that produces repeated proposal variants and enough pain to prove value?</li><li>Which spreadsheet fields are source-of-truth inputs, and which are derived outputs?</li><li>What exact events should retire non-selected options?</li><li>Who must authorize final PDF generation?</li><li>What delivery destinations are required for broker and client?</li><li>What is the required retention/deletion policy after project completion?</li><li>What legacy copy must remain on Medical Captive systems?</li><li>What evidence would make Andrew say this removes real August/September pressure?</li></ol></section>
<section id="10-name-ideas" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">11</div><h2>10 Name Ideas</h2><ol><li>RenewalDesk</li><li>Proposal Lockbox</li><li>Broker Choice Portal</li><li>Renewal Control Room</li><li>FinalOption</li><li>Captive Renewal Hub</li><li>Proposal Source</li><li>RenewalFlow</li><li>BrokerSelect</li><li>Option-to-PDF</li></ol></section>
<section id="next-steps" class="panel section-card"><div class="section-kicker">12</div><h2>Next Steps</h2><ol><li>Turn this brief into a one-page meeting readout for Andrew.</li><li>Build a lightweight renewal flow diagram: input → proposal variants → broker review → selected final → PDF delivery → archive/deletion.</li><li>Create a rough portal mockup with 3 to 5 proposal options and a final PDF button.</li><li>Ask Andrew to pick one representative renewal path for the MVP.</li><li>Confirm retention/deletion commitments and what “bulk erase 45 days after project completion” should mean operationally.</li><li>Bring Kevin into the next call for TPA and broker perspective.</li><li>Decide whether the September 3 follow-up should include static mockup only or a small clickable demo.</li><li>Define a fast proof metric: hours saved per renewal batch, reduction in variant errors, or time from proposal selection to final package sent.</li></ol></section>
